REGULATION (EC) NO 862/2007  
OF THE EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT AND OF THE COUNCIL  
on Community statistics on migration and international protection and repealing Council Regulation (EEC) No 311/76 on the compilation of statistics on foreign workers  

(Text with EEA relevance)  

This Regulation establishes common rules for the collection and compilation of Community statistics on migration and international protection. It repeals Council Regulation (EEC) No 311/76 on the compilation of statistics on foreign workers.  

The Regulation applies to all Member States of the European Union. Its scope covers:  
- Immigration to and emigration from Member State territories, including flows between Member States and between a Member State and a third country;  
- The citizenship and country of birth of persons usually resident in Member States;  
- Administrative and judicial procedures and processes in Member States relating to immigration, granting of permission to reside, citizenship, asylum and other forms of international protection, and the prevention of illegal immigration.  

It does not cover estimates of the number of persons illegally resident in Member States. Member States must not provide such estimates or data to the Commission (Eurostat), although they may include them in population stocks due to surveys.  

The Regulation is based on Council Regulation (EC) No 322/97 on Community Statistics, which sets standards for impartiality, reliability, objectivity, scientific independence, cost-effectiveness and statistical confidentiality.  

The Regulation is implemented through measures adopted by the Commission pursuant to Council Decision 1999/468/EC, including updating definitions, defining data groupings and disaggregations, and laying down accuracy and quality standards. These measures are adopted under the regulatory procedure with scrutiny (Article 5a of Decision 1999/468/EC).  

Key defined terms (as set out in Article 2) include:  
- ‘usual residence’;  
- ‘immigration’;  
- ‘emigration’;  
- ‘citizenship’;  
- ‘country of birth’;  
- ‘immigrant’;  
- ‘emigrant’;  
- ‘long-term resident’ (as defined in Council Directive 2003/109/EC);  
- ‘third-country national’ (any person not a citizen of the Union within the meaning of Article 17(1) of the Treaty, including stateless persons);  
- ‘application for international protection’ (as defined in Council Directive 2004/83/EC);  
- ‘refugee status’ (as defined in Directive 2004/83/EC);  
- ‘subsidiary protection status’ (as defined in Directive 2004/83/EC);  
- ‘family members’ (as defined in Council Regulation (EC) No 343/2003);  
- ‘temporary protection’ (as defined in Council Directive 2001/55/EC);  
- ‘unaccompanied minor’ (as defined in Directive 2004/83/EC);  
- ‘external borders’ (as defined in Regulation (EC) No 562/2006);  
- ‘third-country nationals refused entry’ (those refused entry at an external border due to failure to meet entry conditions or not belonging to specified categories);  
- ‘third-country nationals found to be illegally present’ (those officially found on a Member State’s territory who do not meet stay or residence conditions);  
- ‘resettlement’ (transfer of third-country nationals or stateless persons based on need for international protection and durable solution).  

The Regulation requires Member States to supply statistics to the Commission (Eurostat) on:  
- Immigration, emigration, usual residence, citizenship and country of birth (Article 3);  
- Statistics on international migration, usually resident population and acquisition of citizenship (Article 3);  
- Statistics on international protection (Articles 4, 4(4), 4(5), 4(6), 4(7), 4(8), 4(9), 4(10), 4(11), 4(12), 4(13), 4(14), 4(15), 4(16), 4(17), 4(18), 4(19), 4(20), 4(21), 4(22), 4(23), 4(24), 4(25), 4(26), 4(27), 4(28), 4(29), 4(30), 4(31), 4(32), 4(33), 4(34), 4(35), 4(36), 4(37), 4(38), 4(39), 4(40), 4(41), 4(42), 4(43), 4(44), 4(45), 4(46), 4(47), 4(48), 4(49), 4(50), 4(51), 4(52), 4(53), 4(54), 4(55), 4(56), 4(57), 4(58), 4(59), 4(60), 4(61), 4(62), 4(63), 4(64), 4(65), 4(66), 4(67), 4(68), 4(69), 4(70), 4(71), 4(72), 4(73), 4(74), 4(75), 4(76), 4(77), 4(78), 4(79), 4(80), 4(81), 4(82), 4(83), 4(84), 4(85), 4(86), 4(87), 4(88), 4(89), 4(90), 4(91), 4(92), 4(93), 4(94), 4(95), 4(96), 4(97), 4(98), 4(99), 4(100);  
- Statistics on the prevention of illegal entry and stay (Article 5);  
- Statistics on residence permits and residence of third-country nationals (Article 6);  
- Statistics on returns (Article 7);  
- Additional disaggregations (Article 8);  

All statistics shall be based on appropriate data sources (administrative records, registers, censuses, sample surveys, etc.) and shall comply with quality standards (Article 9). Member States must report on data sources, selection rationale, quality effects, estimation methods, and any revisions or corrections (Article 9).  

Implementation measures (Article 10) are adopted under the regulatory procedure with scrutiny (Article 11(3)) and include updating definitions, defining groupings and disaggregations, defining permit categories, and laying down accuracy and quality standards.  

The Commission shall be assisted by the Statistical Programme Committee (established by Decision 89/382/EEC) in adopting implementing measures (Article 11).  

A report by the Commission on the statistics compiled pursuant to this Regulation and their quality shall be submitted to the European Parliament and the Council by 20 August 2012 and every three years thereafter (Article 12).  

Council Regulation (EEC) No 311/76 is repealed (Article 13).  

This Regulation shall enter into force on the 20th day following its publication in the Official Journal of the European Union.  

This Regulation is binding in its entirety and directly applicable in all Member States.  

Done at Strasbourg, 11 July 2007.  

For the European Parliament  
The President  
H.-G. PÖTTERING  

For the Council  
The President  
M. LOBO ANTUNES

This is a Regulation (EC) No 452/2008 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 23 April 2008, adopting a common framework for the systematic production of Community statistics in the field of education and lifelong learning. Its legal basis is Article 285(1) of the Treaty establishing the European Community.

The Regulation applies to the production of statistics in three domains:  
(a) Domain 1 – statistics on education and training systems;  
(b) Domain 2 – statistics on the participation of adults in lifelong learning;  
(c) Domain 3 – other statistics on education and lifelong learning, such as those on education and the economy, education and the labour market, and education and social inclusion.

The Regulation establishes a common framework for the production of Community statistics in these domains, governed by the Annex, which defines the scope, subjects, characteristics, observation periods, deadlines, and quality requirements for each domain. The production of statistics is carried out through statistical actions, including regular delivery by Member States, use of additional statistical information systems and surveys (particularly for Domain 3), development and improvement of statistical standards and manuals, and improvement of data quality across relevance, accuracy, timeliness, accessibility, comparability, and coherence. Member States are required to take into account regional dimensions and systematically break down data by gender where appropriate. Special attention shall be given to the burden on respondents and educational institutions.

Key defined terms (as per Article 2) are:  
(a) “Community statistics” – defined in the first indent of Article 2 of Regulation (EC) No 322/97;  
(b) “production of statistics” – defined in the second indent of Article 2 of Regulation (EC) No 322/97;  
(c) “national authorities” – defined in the third indent of Article 2 of Regulation (EC) No 322/97;  
(d) “education” – defined as organised and sustained communication designed to bring about learning;  
(e) “lifelong learning” – defined as all learning activity undertaken throughout life, with the aim of improving knowledge, skills and competences within a personal, civic, social and/or employment-related perspective;  
(f) “micro-data” – individual statistical records;  
(g) “confidential data” – data which allow only indirect identification of the statistical units concerned, in accordance with Regulation (EC) No 322/97 and Regulation (Euratom, EEC) No 1588/90.

The Regulation ensures full respect for the right to the protection of personal data as provided for in Article 8 of the Charter of Fundamental Rights of the European Union, and the transmission of confidential micro-data is governed by the rules set out in Regulation (EC) No 322/97 and Council Regulation (Euratom, EEC) No 1588/90.

The measures necessary for implementation shall be adopted in accordance with Council Decision 1999/468/EC of 28 June 1999, specifically through the regulatory procedure with scrutiny provided for in Article 5a of that Decision. The Commission shall be empowered to select and specify the subjects of the statistics, their characteristics, breakdowns, observation periods and deadlines, quality requirements (including precision), and quality reporting framework, adopting these measures in accordance with the regulatory procedure with scrutiny. These measures are intended to amend non-essential elements of the Regulation and are to be adopted as general scope measures, supplementing the Regulation without altering its essential content.

Limited derogations and transition periods for one or more Member States, based on objective grounds, may be adopted in accordance with the regulatory procedure referred to in Article 7(2) of Decision 1999/468/EC.

The Commission shall be assisted by the Statistical Programme Committee established by Council Decision 89/382/EEC, Euratom, in accordance with Article 3 of that Decision.

This Regulation shall enter into force on the 20th day following its publication in the Official Journal of the European Union.

The Regulation is binding in its entirety and directly applicable in all Member States.

This is a Regulation (EC) No 453/2008 of the European Parliament and of the Council of 23 April 2008, acting under Article 251 of the Treaty, with legal basis in Article 285(1) of the Treaty establishing the European Community.

The Regulation establishes requirements for the regular quarterly production of statistics on Community job vacancies. Its scope applies to all Member States, covering job vacancies for business units with one or more employees, broken down by economic activity according to the NACE classification in force, excluding household employment, extraterritorial organisations, and agriculture, forestry and fishing activities (unless provided optionally). It also includes, on an optional basis, data for personal care services (residential care and social work without accommodation). The data shall be provided by Member States using business surveys or other appropriate sources, with optional distinction between fixed-term and permanent vacancies, and shall be seasonally adjusted.

The primary obligations established are:
- Member States shall submit quarterly data on job vacancies, broken down by economic activity (NACE) and covering units with fewer than 10 employees, as determined through feasibility studies;
- Member States shall transmit data and metadata to the Commission (Eurostat) in a specified format and within deadlines determined by the regulatory procedure with scrutiny (Article 9(2));
- Member States shall provide back data for at least the four preceding quarters, with totals delivered no later than the date of first transmission and breakdowns within one year thereafter;
- Member States shall ensure data are relevant, accurate, timely, accessible, comparable, and coherent;
- Member States shall comply with quality assessment dimensions (relevance, accuracy, timeliness, accessibility, comparability, coherence) and submit quality reports as defined by the regulatory procedure;
- The Commission shall adopt feasibility studies for Member States facing difficulties in providing data for small units or specific economic activities (as listed in Article 7), and shall adopt measures based on those studies, respecting cost-effectiveness and minimisation of respondent burden;
- The Commission may establish and coordinate Community sample schemes where national schemes are insufficient, subject to approval and implementation procedures defined by the regulatory procedure;
- Member States may supplement national data with reliable statistical estimation procedures;
- The Regulation may be implemented with financial contribution from the Community for the first three years of data collection, with appropriations fixed annually through budgetary procedures;
- The Regulation shall be published by the Commission (Eurostat) on a quarterly basis, with statistics and analysis accessible to European citizens, particularly via the EURES portal;
- The Regulation shall enter into force on 20 May 2008 (the 20th day following its publication in the Official Journal).

Key defined terms (as per Article 2):
- ‘job vacancy’ means a paid post newly created, unoccupied, or about to become vacant, for which the employer is taking active steps and is prepared to take further steps to find a suitable candidate from outside the enterprise, and which the employer intends to fill either immediately or within a specific period of time (the concepts ‘active steps to find a suitable candidate’ and ‘specific period of time’ shall be defined in accordance with the regulatory procedure with scrutiny referred to in Article 9(2));
- ‘occupied post’ means a paid post within the organisation to which an employee has been assigned;
- ‘metadata’ means the explanations needed to interpret changes in the data arising from either methodological or technical changes;
- ‘back data’ means the historical data covering the specifications given in Article 1.

The Regulation does not contain any transitional or sunset provisions; it is directly applicable in all Member States upon entry into force.

