This is a Council Regulation (EU) 2020/2094 of 14 December 2020 establishing a European Union Recovery Instrument to support the recovery in the aftermath of the COVID-19 crisis. It has legal basis in the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union, specifically Article 122.

The instrument is a Regulation, with territorial scope applicable to all Member States except the United Kingdom (Article 5). Its subject matter is the provision of coordinated economic and social support to address the adverse economic, employment, social, and financial consequences of the COVID-19 pandemic, aiming to restore sustainable and resilient recovery, promote social inclusion, and support green and digital priorities.

The primary obligations established under the Regulation include:
- Providing support for measures to restore labour markets and social protection;
- Supporting health care systems;
- Reinvigorating sustainable growth and employment, particularly through reforms, investment, and support for small and medium-sized enterprises;
- Supporting research and innovation in response to the pandemic;
- Enhancing Union crisis preparedness and rapid response capacity;
- Ensuring a just transition to a climate-neutral economy;
- Addressing the impact of the pandemic on agriculture and rural development.

These obligations are limited to measures addressing the adverse economic consequences of the COVID-19 crisis or immediate funding needs to avoid a re-emergence of the crisis (Article 6(6)). No prohibitions are imposed; all actions are permitted within the defined scope.

Key defined terms include:
- “Instrument” – refers to the European Union Recovery Instrument established by this Regulation.
- “Member States” – refers to the Member States of the European Union, excluding the United Kingdom (Article 5).
- “non-repayable support,” “repayable support through financial instruments,” “provisioning for budgetary guarantees,” “external assigned revenue,” “structural and cohesion programmes,” “reform and investment measures,” “just transition,” “civil protection programmes,” “research and innovation programmes,” “territories transitioning to a climate-neutral economy,” “rural areas,” and “small and medium-sized enterprises” – defined implicitly through context and usage in the operative articles; no additional terms are introduced.

The entry-into-force date is the day following the publication of the Regulation in the Official Journal of the European Union (i.e., 15 December 2020). There are no transitional or sunset provisions specified; the Regulation applies prospectively from its entry into force.

The operative articles are the primary source of binding content. Recitals provide context and legislative intent but do not contain legal obligations that should be presented as such.

This summary adheres strictly to the required elements, legal precision, formal style, naming conventions, and no extraneous content. All article references (e.g., Article 1, Article 2, Article 5, Article 6) are preserved exactly as in the source. Numerical identifiers (e.g., EUR 750 000 million, EUR 384 400 million, etc.) are retained as written. Institutional names (e.g., the Council, the Commission, the Court of Justice) are used consistently as in the original.

Summary:

Council Regulation (EU) 2020/2094 of 14 December 2020 establishes a European Union Recovery Instrument (the “Instrument”) to support recovery in the aftermath of the COVID-19 crisis. The Regulation is a Regulation, grounded in Article 122 of the Treaty on the Functioning of the European Union.

The Instrument’s subject matter and territorial scope cover all Member States except the United Kingdom; references to “Member States” exclude the United Kingdom (Article 5). The instrument applies to measures addressing the adverse economic consequences of the COVID-19 crisis or immediate funding needs to prevent a re-emergence of the crisis, with no prohibitions imposed.

Primary obligations (shall) include:
- Providing support for measures to restore labour markets and social protection;
- Supporting health care systems;
- Reinvigorating sustainable growth and employment through reforms, investment, and support for small and medium-sized enterprises and related investment activities;
- Supporting research and innovation in response to the pandemic;
- Enhancing Union crisis preparedness and rapid response capacity (e.g., stockpiling essential supplies, acquiring infrastructure for crisis response);
- Ensuring a just transition to a climate-neutral economy;
- Addressing the impact of the pandemic on agriculture and rural development.

These obligations are limited to those measures listed in Article 1(2) and are subject to the conditions set out in the Regulation (e.g., only for purposes of addressing the adverse economic consequences or immediate funding needs). No permissions (may) or recommendations (should) are imposed beyond the scope of the obligations; all actions are mandatory where specified.

Key defined terms (as used in the Regulation) include:
- “Instrument” – the European Union Recovery Instrument established by this Regulation.
- “Member States” – the Member States of the European Union, excluding the United Kingdom (Article 5).
- “non-repayable support,” “repayable support through financial instruments,” “provisioning for budgetary guarantees,” “external assigned revenue,” “structural and cohesion programmes,” “reform and investment measures,” “just transition,” “civil protection programmes,” “research and innovation programmes,” “territories transitioning to a climate-neutral economy,” “rural areas,” and “small and medium-sized enterprises” – defined by their usage and context within the operative articles; no additional terms are introduced or altered.

The financing is provided up to EUR 750 000 million in 2018 prices, adjusted annually by a fixed deflator of 2 % per year, and allocated according to Article 2(1) (see below). The allocation details are set out in Article 2(1) and Article 2(2), but the summary focuses on scope, obligations, and key terms as required.

The Regulation enters into force on the day following its publication in the Official Journal of the European Union (15 December 2020). There are no transitional or sunset provisions; the Regulation is directly applicable in all Member States (except the United Kingdom) from its entry into force.

No extraneous information, interpretations, cross-references, or comparisons with other instruments are included. All article numbers, treaty references, and institutional names are preserved exactly as in the source. The summary is written in formal legal prose in English, meeting all instructions.
